<h2>The headline: DORB export is open again</h2>
<p>If you tried to source de-oiled rice bran (DORB) from India at any point between mid-2023 and October 2025 and came away empty-handed, or saw published trade statistics that looked implausibly small for a product India produces at real scale, there's a simple explanation: export was prohibited. DGFT Notification No. 37/2025-26, effective 3 October 2025, lifted that prohibition.</p>

<h2>Why it was banned</h2>
<p>DORB is a significant cattle-feed ingredient within India's own dairy and livestock sector, and the export restriction — in place from July 2023 — was implemented to protect domestic supply and keep feed costs manageable for Indian farmers. This is a recurring pattern in Indian agricultural export policy: when a commodity is important to domestic food or feed security, export restrictions are a tool the government uses relatively readily, sometimes with limited advance notice.</p>

<h2>Don't read 2024 trade data as market demand</h2>
<p>This is worth stating plainly because it's an easy mistake to make. Calendar-2024 export figures for DORB — verified through UN Comtrade — show total exports of just USD 417,150, an almost negligible number for a product of DORB's normal production scale. That figure reflects the export ban being in force for the entire year, not weak demand or limited Indian supply capacity. Post-ban trade data for 2026 hadn't yet published in full at the time we researched this, so the real post-liberalisation trade picture is still emerging.</p>

<h2>What this means practically</h2>
<p>If DORB is relevant to your feed formulation, this is a genuine reopening of a supply option, not a marginal development. We can now quote DORB for export, subject to the same specification and documentation process as any other feed ingredient — protein content, oil/fat residual, moisture and fibre content are the parameters to specify.</p>

<h2>A word of caution for planning purposes</h2>
<p>Indian export policy on feed and food commodities has shown a pattern of changing with limited notice — this ban itself, and its lifting, are both examples. We'd recommend confirming current DORB export status at the time of any order rather than assuming the current Free status is permanent, the same caution we'd apply to onion, wheat or any other policy-sensitive product.</p>
